The first one which is the neural net
it contains 3000 neurons

Each neuron has a state
1 or 0 or -1
which is closer to ternary than binary
this makes more sense since a binary can technically hold "fire" and "rest"
while 3 states make it closer to how a real neuron works in technical terms
now at the MAX state it uses near 2bits to 1 byte

which means even if you scale to near 100k it would use like near a MB of RAM while a modern 
Matrice use more than lets use 9x9 in this example

as a single multiplication, the answer is 81 and it uses near 12 bytes of RAM
but as a matrice it uses 972bytes

At the WORSE case scenario , 3888 Neurons could use at 972 or near 
that number BUT that is a Static answer since it assumes all 3888 neurons use 
 the highest value which is 1 and that is usually NEVER EVER the case unless you have a 
 small amount of neurons like 4 or 8 but other than that she is usually maxxing out at 972 bytes BUT she is 
 averagely lesser than that so a good estimate is usually 600 to 800 atmost but there is no way 
 in the world it can get THAT high

anyways
since we layed out the large array of the neural net
now we need to 'teach' it.

when you learn something it becomes a ton of biased data. but in fancy terms it is called Synaptic Weights

its when a Neuron pairs with another Neuron and fire and do a cascade.
BUT it needs to have a Reference data or something that makes it hold something then output something else again.

that is the 'data'
now imagine a python file that makes a file or binary with a ton of 'synaptic weights' for the neural net. thats the python trainer.
easy
we got the 'teach' part done

now we need an output system

this is where we take specific patterns of neurons firing in specific cascades
this is where it gets kinda tricky

we have to give it a full QWERTY upper and lowercase with punctuation and symbols AND numbers
then we need to map it to a text interface and then we need to map Input towards the neural net and pull neural data/firing back to as output

this CAN get ttrickky really fast when mapping it..

now that being said 

we need to add the wifi wikapedia 

we just make it as "more synaptic weights" where as when the wifi stack pulls up the info that has been cleansed of bloat, they count as "More temporary synaptic weights"

that way she learns something quickly, but its only a temporary item.

that is her core system

other stuff like TGSL (Text generation safety layer) catching bad words is easy, just  hook a dictionary of strings and queries similar to harmful items and boom its done

